  "2026-07-05T08:02:33.747Z",codex-sdk-agent,"Scope alignment evidence: PR #431 review caught that the target-surface body omitted append from item facets. Updated the live epic body through pm update so the consolidation grammar includes comments/notes/learnings/files/docs/deps/append/test."
  "2026-07-06T22:23:28.633Z",codex,"Audit evidence 2026-07-06/07 codex: live pm --help still exposes 64+ top-level/user-visible spellings plus extension commands and the internal test-runs-worker command. Existing child set covers list/history/package/lifecycle/calendar/facets/context/ops/aggregate/customization/help/contracts/alias-shim clusters. Duplicate-safe search found no focused item for the internal worker leakage, so pm-lp4j was created under this epic. This is a simplification gap only; no runtime feature is removed."
  "2026-07-06T22:31:27.154Z",codex-agent,"Planning evidence 2026-07-06/07 codex-agent: live contracts expose 101 command paths and 59 top-level spellings before consolidation. Existing child partition still covers the simplification strategy: ADR pm-pbyu, token baseline pm-a22j, alias shim pm-0z7n, list/history/package/lifecycle/calendar/facet/context/ops/aggregate/customization clusters, tiered help pm-e2bq, contracts/docs generation pm-mu8m, and internal-worker leakage pm-lp4j. Recommended planning stance: accept the ADR first, land alias shim second, then migrate one cluster at a time with spelling-parity tests and zero feature loss; do not remove any old spelling until hidden aliases and migration hints are test-gated."
  "2026-07-08T16:41:53.189Z",claude-code-agent,"Bundle evidence: contracts --summary provides a cheap top-level command grammar bootstrap for progressive disclosure before agents request command-scoped flags."
  "2026-07-08T17:07:59.070Z",claude-code-agent,"Verification evidence: full coverage passed at 100/100/100/100 (5462 tests), lint/static/docs/contracts gates passed, and packed-install temp proof passed in /tmp/pm-agent-ux-proof-TYSgf1 for pm list --today, pm list --recent, pm contracts --summary, and SDK runAction({action:'contracts', options:{summary:true}})."
  "2026-07-08T17:41:49.351Z",claude-code-agent,"Evidence update: CI Gates (static) failed because the committed contract snapshot was generated before rebuilding dist after tool-parameter metadata copy changes. Rebuilt with pnpm build, regenerated with pnpm contracts:update, and reran the full static CI sequence locally: pnpm version:check && pnpm changelog:pm:check && pnpm contracts:check && pnpm security:scan && pnpm lint && npm pack --dry-run. Result: passed."
  "2026-07-08T18:04:30.464Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence: addressed latest bot feedback before repush. Fixed SDK/MCP search contract leakage by keeping list-only --today/--recent out of search schemas, added help to contracts summary intents, treated programmatic updatedAfter:null as unselected, and clarified open-only today docs. Verification passed: focused tests (78 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5463 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T18:23:03.452Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed the final Gemini normalizer finding by making normalizeExtensionFlagName accept unknown, ignore null/blank values, trim valid strings, and reject non-string values with a deterministic TypeError. Added focused coverage. Verification passed: focused tests (79 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5464 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T18:49:40.069Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed final Gemini edge cases by ignoring blank updatedAfter values in list window conflict detection and adding more core command intents (guide, ops, reindex, focus, telemetry) to contracts summary output. Verification passed: focused tests (79 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5464 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T19:17:16.701Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed CodeRabbit schema feedback by expanding the shared summary parameter description to include health rollups and adding contracts action schema mutual-exclusion rules for projection flags. Verification passed: focused tests (80 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5465 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T19:39:00.993Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed Greptile pushed-review schema/runtime mismatch by adding action-schema mutual-exclusion rules for list today/recent/updatedAfter windows across list variants. Verification passed: focused tests (81 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5466 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T20:07:39.770Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed final value-sensitive schema feedback by making schema mutual exclusion conditional on selected boolean values, adding completion summary intent, and rejecting bare '-' short extension flags. Verification passed: focused tests (81 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5466 tests)."
  "2026-07-13T21:41:29.812Z",codex-root,"Command-surface audit 2026-07-13: the live root help advertises 67 subcommands. They partition into history 6, list/query 9, item facets 8, package-owned governance/audit 6, claim lifecycle 4, close lifecycle 4, package lifecycle 4, context 3, calendar sugar 3, operations 7, stable remaining core 12, and one internal worker leak. Existing children already cover the consolidation work, so no duplicate simplification items were created. Proposed target remains a small noun-oriented grammar with status filters replacing list aliases, item facets grouped under item, lifecycle actions grouped consistently, package/extension commands grouped under package, and extension-owned audit commands discoverable without polluting the core surface. Preserve every capability, stable machine-readable contracts, shell composability, and compact default output."
  "2026-07-13T22:09:18.183Z",codex-root,"Relationship organization 2026-07-13: agent story pm-1kxs now has blocked_by edges to all 15 active command-grammar delivery clusters required by its acceptance criteria: alias foundation, tiered help, list/history/package/lifecycle/calendar/bulk/navigation/facets/ops/aggregate/customization consolidation, internal-worker hiding, and contract-generated adapters/docs. Metrics-only pm-0mox remains non-blocking. This records the complete delivery graph without creating any duplicate simplification item."
  "2026-07-15T20:06:34.985Z",codex-audit-20260715,"Review pass 91 agent-UX evidence: pm plan create executes as a positional action, but pm plan create --help --json returns unknown_command while pm plan --help advertises create and the help model exposes no subcommands. Focused issue pm-2tan was added under the command/help lineage to unify action help, contracts, and completion without broadening this umbrella."
  "2026-07-21T15:56:01.477Z",codex-tracker-governance,"Live command-surface planning 2026-07-21: root help exposes 75 top-level names while contracts summary exposes 63 canonical summaries. The partition remains complete with zero feature loss: list variants -> pm-pfqi; history and restore family -> pm-tqel; package lifecycle -> pm-tnud; lifecycle sugar -> pm-eq4x; calendar sugar -> pm-o3fh; bulk/destructive -> pm-ik19; navigation/context -> pm-kcs4; item facets -> pm-yql1; diagnostics -> pm-6apl; aggregate -> pm-xkgq; customization -> pm-npr3. pm-lp4j owns test-runs-worker visibility and pm-szdc owns completion helper visibility. item, graph, merge, plan, package, schema, config, and history are noun-verb exemplars, not new leaks. Sequence remains decision pm-pbyu, alias substrate pm-0z7n, independent cluster migrations, tiered help pm-e2bq, generated contracts/docs pm-mu8m, then token regression proof pm-cu1i."
  "2026-07-24T12:05:47.149Z",claude-code-agent,"Current agent-surface evidence: root help exposes 75 top-level names while contracts summary exposes 63 canonical summaries. Default TOON remains the token-efficient baseline, but representative real-tracker payloads are still substantial: context --limit 10 about 7.6 KB / 793 words, search --limit 20 about 7.3 KB / 780 words, list-open --limit 20 about 3.6 KB / 419 words, and get brief about 2.3 KB / 289 words. The existing noun-verb consolidation, tiered help, generated contract/docs, and token-budget children fully own this; no parallel simplification item was created."
  "2026-07-26T10:30:05.389Z","harness:opencode","Command-surface census 2026-07-26 (audit plan pm-t9e3bc): live contracts summary reports 74 top-level command spellings (63 core + 11 extension-provided: beads, cal, calendar, changelog, command-kit, comments-audit, dedupe-audit, dedupe-merge, guide, normalize, reindex, search-advanced, templates, test-runs, todos, twin, vcs — 17 with the 6 deduped calendar/changelog alias pairs); pm --help renders 88 spelling lines including alias pairs (context|ctx, package|packages). Surface is stable vs the 75-name 2026-07-25 census (no growth pressure this week), confirming consolidation remains the binding constraint rather than surface expansion. contracts --flags-only payload measured at 117,070 bytes in this extension-loaded workspace."
  "2026-07-26T10:30:55.904Z","harness:opencode","Census precision correction (same pass): the verifiable split is 74 top-level spellings in contracts --summary and 88 spelling lines in pm --help including alias pairs; the runtime profile reports 7 extensions loaded contributing 21 command handlers and 7 parser overrides. The core/extension noun arithmetic in my previous comment was illustrative and mis-added — treat the 74/88/7-loaded/21-handler numbers as the census of record. Conclusion unchanged: surface stable vs the 75-name census, consolidation (not growth) remains the binding constraint."
notes[21]:
  - created_at: "2026-07-05T04:55:44.581Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Brainstorm census 2026-07-05 — current surface is ~60 top-level commands. Redundancy clusters found; 8 list-* status variants (list/all/draft/open/in-progress/blocked/closed/canceled), 5 history-family commands (history/history-redact/history-repair/history-compact/activity) plus restore, 4 package-lifecycle commands (extension/package/install/upgrade — extension and package are already partial synonyms), 3 lifecycle sugar commands (start-task/pause-task/close-task) duplicating claim/release/close+status, 3 calendar shortcuts (meet/event/remind) duplicating create with presets, 6 diagnostics commands (stats/health/validate/gc/telemetry/eval). Consolidating just these clusters removes ~24 top-level entries with zero feature loss."
  - created_at: "2026-07-05T04:55:44.951Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Sequencing decision — alias shim layer (pm-0z7n) is the shared foundation and must land first after ADR pm-pbyu acceptance; each cluster task then becomes a mechanical table edit + spelling-parity spec. Token baseline (pm-a22j) runs before any change so the epic has measurable before/after evidence. Every rename must walk the full wiring checklist (commands/index, register-mutation, mcp/server, sdk/cli-contracts, contracts.ts, completions x3 shells, docs, vitest.config, contracts:update)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-06T08:04:41.835Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "2026-07-06 audit #7 (third pass): live top-level surface measured at 64 commands (pm --help). All previously-filed clusters still map 1:1 to children. Two unmapped spellings found and resolved by note/comment (no new items needed): 'aggregate' -> proposed as a pm list projection flag (query-read family, see ADR comment on pm-pbyu); 'test-all' -> ops verb, scope note added to pm-6apl. 'beads' and 'changelog' are package-owned commands and already conform to grammar rule 4 (sugar/package-provided, not core surface). New child added: pm-vxxm (GH-442 contracts --summary) — the contracts-side tier of the token-cost AC. Fresh dogfood evidence quantifying the epic's motivation now tracked under pm-mpbb: pm-cfed (JSON +48-53% vs TOON), pm-nilh (mutation echo 12.7x), pm-ishm (43% of context = repeated tags), pm-g9xk (constant error boilerplate)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-06T23:10:33.100Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "2026-07-07 grammar re-verification + brainstorm additions. Live surface counted from pm --help: 65 top-level commands (63 core + 2 extension groups beads/changelog). Every core spelling maps to exactly one consolidation child (pm-pfqi list-*, pm-tqel history family, pm-tnud package family, pm-eq4x lifecycle sugar, pm-o3fh calendar sugar, pm-ik19 bulk/destructive, pm-kcs4 navigation, pm-yql1 annotation facets incl. test/test-all, pm-6apl ops, pm-xkgq aggregate, pm-npr3 customization family, pm-lp4j internal worker) — partition still complete, no new top-level command has leaked in since the last pass. New brainstorm points: (1) fix a SHARED VERB VOCABULARY across nouns so agents can guess subcommands (proposal commented on ADR pm-pbyu); (2) future query-power features must land as flags on existing nouns, never new nouns — filed pm-plwm (--where predicates) with that constraint stated in its AC; (3) tie the done-definition to the pm-a22j token baseline: target pm --help fitting one screen and contracts --summary (pm-vxxm) as the default agent bootstrap path."
  - created_at: "2026-07-18T05:46:18.034Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Live grammar exemplar recorded 2026-07-18: `pm graph` (PR#580/#581) is the first large post-census command family shipped natively in the target noun-verb shape — ONE top-level noun with 12 verbs (neighbors/paths/closure/impact/subgraph/audit/profile/analyze/communities/redundancy/dominators/plan), subcommand enum single-sourced in sdk/cli-contracts/enum-contracts.ts, thin 7-line CLI shim (cli/graph.ts), one MCP tool. Net top-level surface cost of an entire graph platform: +1 command. This is the concrete before/after argument for the pbyu decision and the consolidation children (pfqi list-*, tqel history, tnud package, eq4x lifecycle): each cluster collapses N spellings to one noun exactly like graph. Also the counter-pressure evidence: every new first-class MCP tool costs ~+500 cold-start tokens (measurement on pm-9k90), so consolidation + profiles beat tool-count growth."
  - created_at: "2026-07-18T14:08:36.887Z"
    author: codex-ecosystem-planner
    text: "2026-07-18 live command-surface census and complete partition review: root help exposes 73 names. User capability still partitions without loss into the existing noun families: list variants -> pm-pfqi; history/activity/restore/repair/redact/compact -> pm-tqel; package/extension/install/upgrade -> pm-tnud; update-many/close-many/delete/test-all -> pm-ik19; context/next/focus -> pm-kcs4; comments/notes/learnings/files/docs/deps/append/test -> pm-yql1; meet/event/remind -> pm-o3fh; start/pause/close-task -> pm-eq4x; stats/health/validate/gc/telemetry/eval -> pm-6apl; aggregate -> pm-xkgq; init/config/schema/profile -> pm-npr3. graph is the conforming noun-verb exemplar; beads/changelog/copy/guide are extension-owned surfaces; completion is the public completion noun. Internal leakage is now fully owned by pm-lp4j for test-runs-worker and new pm-szdc for completion-statuses/completion-tags/completion-types. Delivery sequence remains pbyu decision -> 0z7n alias substrate -> cluster migrations, while e2bq + mu8m + lp4j + szdc independently shrink and single-source discovery. No other command lacks a canonical target."
  - created_at: "2026-07-24T23:56:26.891Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Live surface census 2026-07-25: 75 top-level commands (pm --help), unchanged in shape since the last census but grown by the graph, plan, merge, and history-* families. Target-grammar brainstorm re-run against that surface; the existing partition still covers every command, and this note records the resulting noun table so the ADR can be decided against something concrete rather than a principle.\n\nProposed twelve-noun target grammar (verbs are shared vocabulary; every current spelling survives as a hidden alias):\n\n1. item — create, copy, update, close, delete, restore, claim, release, focus. Absorbs: create, copy, update, update-many, close, close-many, delete, restore, claim, release, focus, start-task, pause-task, close-task, item. Bulk is a flag (--ids/--where), not a command; owners pm-ik19, pm-eq4x.\n2. list — status and filter variants collapse to flags. Absorbs the eight list-* commands plus aggregate via --group-by/--count-by; owners pm-pfqi, pm-xkgq.\n3. context — next and focus become verbs of the navigation noun. Absorbs context/ctx, next, focus; owner pm-kcs4.\n4. search — search plus eval as a verb (search eval). Owner: retrieval epic.\n5. get — get, history read, and point-in-time reads (--at). Read-only single-item projection.\n6. note — the annotation facet: comments, notes, learnings, files, docs, deps, append, test. One facet grammar; owner pm-yql1.\n7. graph — already a noun with fifteen subcommands; absorbs deps traversal views.\n8. plan — already a noun.\n9. history — history, activity, restore-adjacent maintenance, redact, repair, compact, verify. Owner pm-tqel.\n10. package — extension, package, install, upgrade. Owner pm-tnud.\n11. workspace — init, config, schema, profile. Open question owned by pm-npr3: one customization noun or four peers.\n12. ops — stats, health, validate, gc, telemetry, eval, test-all, contracts. Owner pm-6apl.\n\nRemoved from the default surface entirely (not from the product): completion-* helper entrypoints (pm-szdc), test-runs-worker (pm-lp4j), meet/event/remind moved into the calendar package as create sugar (pm-o3fh).\n\nRatio: 75 to 12 top-level nouns, with zero capability removed. That is the number the ADR should either accept or amend.\n\nTwo grammar rules that make the table learnable without documentation, proposed as ADR content:\n- Shared verb vocabulary: the same verb means the same thing on every noun (add, remove, list, show, set, run). An agent that learns one noun can guess the rest.\n- Scope precedes verb, consistently: the config family already established `pm config project set <key>`, and the same ordering should hold anywhere a scope exists, so there is one rule to remember rather than a per-command convention.\n\nAcceptance target for the outcome milestone: the core surface renders in one screen, and the golden-transcript replay shows a measured drop in tokens per completed task against the pre-consolidation baseline."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T06:21:59.265Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Shared verb vocabulary, drafted 2026-07-25 as the missing half of the twelve-noun table in the prior note. The noun table alone does not make the surface guessable: an agent that learns `pm item update` still cannot predict `pm note add` unless the verbs mean one thing everywhere. This note proposes the closed verb set the ADR should ratify, and it is deliberately small - eleven verbs cover all 76 current spellings.\n\nRead verbs: `show` (one subject, full projection), `list` (many subjects, filtered projection), `find` (ranked retrieval over a query rather than a filter). Write verbs: `add` (append a member to a collection), `set` (replace a scalar or a whole collection), `remove` (delete a member from a collection), `new` (create a subject), `drop` (delete a subject). Process verbs: `run` (execute something with side effects outside the workspace), `check` (evaluate and report without mutating), `fix` (evaluate and repair, always with a dry-run form).\n\nThree rules make the set learnable. First, one verb has one meaning on every noun: `add` never creates a subject and `new` never appends to a collection, which is the ambiguity that makes the current `create` versus `append` versus `--add` split unguessable. Second, scope precedes verb wherever a scope exists, as the config family already established. Third, plurality lives in the noun, never in the verb: `pm item list` and never `pm items list`, so an agent that guesses a plural gets a precise alias hint instead of an unknown-command error.\n\nMapping the current surface onto this set is mechanical and reveals the real inconsistencies rather than hiding them: `create` becomes `item new`; `update` becomes `item set`; `update-many` becomes `item set --where`; `close`/`claim`/`release` stay as lifecycle verbs on `item` because they are domain transitions rather than generic mutations, and the ADR should say so explicitly rather than forcing them into `set`; `comments`/`notes`/`learnings`/`files`/`docs`/`deps`/`test` become facet nouns under `note`-style facet grammar with `add`/`list`/`remove`/`set`; `validate`/`health`/`stats` become `ops check` variants; `gc`/`normalize`/`history-repair` become `ops fix` variants with mandatory dry-run; `search` becomes `find`; the eight `list-*` spellings become `list --status`.\n\nTwo exceptions are worth naming so they are decided rather than discovered. Lifecycle transitions (`claim`, `release`, `close`) read better as verbs than as `set --status`, and they carry side effects that `set` should not imply; the recommendation is to keep them and document them as the one closed set of domain verbs. And `graph`, `plan`, and `history` already carry rich internal subcommand vocabularies that predate this set; the recommendation is to grandfather their existing subcommands as noun-local verbs and require only that new additions come from the shared set, which is exactly what the conformance gate filed as pm-wt43zj can enforce.\n\nAcceptance framing for the ADR: the vocabulary is ratified as data in the contracts table, not as prose in a document, so that the gate can enforce it and the completion, help, and MCP generators can all read the same source. The measurable claim is that an agent given only the noun list and the verb list can construct a valid invocation for a capability it has never seen, which pm-8pnj's transcript replay can test directly by removing help output from the harness."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T17:54:05.849Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface census measured on the shipped build 2026-07-25 (version 2026.7.25) — the baseline this epic must move, and the number to re-measure after each consolidation lands.\n\n- 77 top-level commands (was 78 at the last count; `pm --help` renders them in 12,281 bytes, roughly 3k tokens before an agent has issued a single useful call).\n- 1,712 total flag declarations across those 77 commands.\n- 470 distinct flag spellings.\n- Worst per-command flag counts: plan 97, update 82, create 78, list 66, search 58, test 28, context 25, schema 22, config 18.\n- Subcommand-bearing families: extension 15, package 15, merge 5, config 5, plan 4, graph 3, telemetry 3, schema 2, profile 2, item 2, history 2.\n- Most-repeated flag spellings: --author 35 commands, --message 30, --compact 28, --force 27, --limit 25, --priority 23, --parent 23, --type 21, --sprint 20, --release 20, --assignee 19.\n\nTwo observations that bear on sequencing rather than on any single child:\n\n1. The consolidation children cover every one of the 77 commands (verified by walking the list against pfqi/tnud/tqel/kcs4/ik19/o3fh/eq4x/yql1/xkgq/npr3/6apl/szdc/lp4j), so the epic's decomposition is complete — no unowned command remains. What is not yet covered anywhere is the flag axis: 1,712 declarations behind 77 commands is the larger share of what an agent must model, and collapsing 77 nouns to ~15 does nothing to it by itself. pm-fs8q9x is the only owner of that axis; it should not be treated as optional polish after the noun work.\n\n2. `--author` appearing on 35 commands is a surface-level echo of pm-sx52hr: the flag an agent is mandated never to type is the single most-repeated flag in the CLI, and every one of those 35 declarations is also a help-text line an agent reads."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T06:04:16.753Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface census extension 2026-07-26: the census has been counting two of three surfaces.\n\nBoth prior censuses measured commands and flags. Measured today on the shipped 2026.7.26 build, the third surface is larger than either governed vocabulary:\n\n- 77 top-level commands, contracted in pm contracts, governed by this epic's noun grammar and pm-wt43zj's conformance gate.\n- 470 distinct flag spellings across 1,712 declarations, contracted in the flag-contract tables, governed by pm-fs8q9x's one-spelling-per-concept rule and per-command budget.\n- 195 distinct error codes, contracted nowhere, governed by nothing. `pm contracts --json | jq keys` returns fourteen keys and not one of them enumerates a code; grepping docs for a representative code returns nothing.\n\nThe sequencing consequence for this epic. Consolidating 77 nouns to 12 reduces what an agent must read to find a command. It does not touch what an agent must read when the command fails, and the failure path is where unplanned tokens are spent - an agent that guesses wrong pays the error output plus a retry, and today it cannot pre-learn the taxonomy that would let it retry correctly the first time. Filed as pm-x4nn3z under this epic so the consolidation is measured against all three surfaces rather than two.\n\nTwo live defects found in the same review make the argument without needing a hypothetical, and both are the kind that an enumeration exposes on sight rather than by report:\n\n- pm-yqe0mo: the cross-command flag hint caps its candidate list at three with no truncation marker and no relevance ranking, so `pm deps --add` reports that --add is valid on comments, notes and learnings while omitting files, docs and test - the omitted set containing the command actually wanted. Four lines of root cause in commander-usage.ts.\n- pm-cyrfjq: commands with mutually exclusive output modes emit both collection keys and zero-fill the inactive one, so `pm history --json` reports count 7 alongside `history: []`. The same shape was already filed once for pm activity (pm-p3x4), which means the generator is a missing envelope rule rather than two coincidences.\n\nNeither is a grammar defect, so neither would have been caught by the noun-verb work; both are surface-consistency defects of exactly the kind this epic exists to eliminate, on the surface the epic has not yet claimed.\n\nRevision to the acceptance framing, proposed rather than applied. The epic's current acceptance target is \"the core surface renders in one screen\" plus a measured drop in tokens per completed task. The first half only constrains surface one. Suggest the ADR state the target as three governed vocabularies - nouns, flags, codes - each with an enumeration, a stability rule, and a gate, because a one-screen command list sitting above 470 ungoverned flag spellings and 195 ungoverned codes has moved the learning cost rather than removed it. The tokens-per-completed-task metric (pm-8pnj) already measures the right thing and will show the difference either way; the point of stating it in the ADR is to stop the epic from declaring victory on the noun count alone."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T09:55:10.350Z"
    author: "harness:opencode"
    text: "## Brainstorm 2026-07-26 — noun-verb consolidation, grounded in the live 87-command surface\n\nVerified live today: **87 top-level commands** (ADR pm-pbyu recorded ~60 — the surface grew ~45% while the ADR sat open). This is the strongest evidence that the grammar decision cannot wait: every new feature still adds a top-level entry by default.\n\n### Complete command-by-command mapping to the target grammar\n\n| Current (87) | Target noun/verb | Facet |\n|---|---|---|\n| init | init | — |\n| config | config | — |\n| extension, package, install, upgrade | package | lifecycle verbs as subcommands/flags |\n| list, list-all, list-draft, list-open, list-in-progress, list-blocked, list-closed, list-canceled | list | `--status <s>` (8 spellings → 1 flag; pm-pfqi) |\n| aggregate | list | `--group-by/--count-by` (pm-xkgq) |\n| context, ctx, next, focus | context | `next`/`focus` verbs (pm-kcs4) |\n| search, search-advanced | search | advanced flags fold into base |\n| eval | ops | diagnostic (ADR-owned resolution) |\n| get | get | — |\n| history, activity, restore | history | verbs (pm-tqel) |\n| history-redact, history-repair, history-author-acknowledge, history-compact | history | verbs (pm-tqel) |\n| events | history | `history events` (stream is history-derived) or ops |\n| graph | graph | stays — first-class noun (pm-96h7) |\n| create, copy | create | `create --from <id>` |\n| meet, event, remind, cal, calendar | package-owned | builtin-calendar package (pm-o3fh) |\n| update, update-many | update | `--all/--where` bulk facets (pm-ik19) |\n| close, close-many, delete | close | bulk + destructive facets (pm-ik19) |\n| item, comments, notes, learnings, files, docs, deps, append, test, test-all, test-runs | item | annotation facets (pm-yql1); test-runs worker hidden (pm-lp4j) |\n| plan | plan | — |\n| claim, release, start-task, pause-task, close-task | claim | `--release`, lifecycle sugar hidden (pm-eq4x) |\n| telemetry, stats, health, validate, gc, normalize, reindex, comments-audit, dedupe-audit, dedupe-merge | ops | diagnostics/governance verbs (pm-6apl) |\n| contracts | contracts | — |\n| schema, templates | schema | templates = per-type create defaults |\n| profile | profile | — |\n| merge | merge | stays (git-driver lifecycle) or ops verb |\n| changelog | ops or package-owned | generated artifact |\n| guide | help | `help guide <topic>` |\n| completion, completion-statuses, completion-tags, completion-types | hidden | runtime helpers, never in help (pm-szdc) |\n| beads, todos, command-kit, twin, vcs | package-owned nouns | extension commands, not core surface |\n| help | help | — |\n\nResult: **~20 core spellings** (init, config, schema, profile, package, create, get, list, update, close, claim, item, history, search, plan, context, graph, ops, contracts, help) + package-owned nouns. Every removed spelling keeps working through the pm-0z7n alias shim with migration hints and pm-0mox usage counters.\n\n### Principles to strengthen in ADR pm-pbyu\n\n1. **One concept, one spelling** — the flag lexicon gate (pm-fs8q9x) must be part of the grammar, not a follow-up: grammar freeze without spelling freeze leaves 87-ish concept-variants alive behind flags.\n2. **The error surface is part of the grammar** — 195 uncontracted error codes (pm-x4nn3z) are what agents actually parse when wrong; consolidation that only renames success paths misses half the cognitive cost. Recovery bundles (pm-ikv6m0, pm-yqe0mo, pm-5h9g, pm-ulqu) ship with the noun that owns them.\n3. **Token cost is the fitness function, not command count** — the measurable acceptance target lives on pm-a22j's baseline + pm-cu1i's ratchet: a grammar that fits one screen AND costs fewer tokens per completed task (pm-8pnj transcript replay). Command count is a proxy; tokens-per-task is the goal.\n4. **Cold-start is two calls** — `pm contracts --summary` + `pm next` (pm-ka6d) must stay true after consolidation; tiered help (pm-e2bq) and single-tiering (pm-kxci8x) derive from the same contract table.\n5. **Grammar serves the philosophy** — fewer spellings = less of the agent's context window spent on *operating the tool*, more on *the project's context*. project management = context management applies to the CLI itself.\n\n### Suggested sequencing (feeds pm-j82fd3, deadline 2026-10-31)\n\nADR accept → pm-0z7n shim layer → pm-0mox counters → noun clusters in dependency order (list pm-pfqi first: pure flag projection, zero mutation risk) → claim sugar (pm-eq4x) → item facets (pm-yql1) → history (pm-tqel) → bulk (pm-ik19) → ops (pm-6apl) → package (pm-tnud) → calendar extraction (pm-o3fh) → aggregate (pm-xkgq) → init/config/schema/profile mapping (pm-npr3) → context trio (pm-kcs4) → gates pm-wt43zj/pm-fs8q9x flip from baseline to enforce."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T10:50:04.507Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Target-surface census 2026-07-26 (88 top-level spellings live, including package-owned): five CORE nouns have no placement in the target table and three of them shipped after the table was written. Unplaced: graph (11 subcommands — ancestors, descendants, predecessors, successors, paths, impact, analyze, audit, communities, redundancy, dominators, slack, centrality, articulation, plan, index — shipped 2026-07-17 via PR#580), merge (merge install, shipped in the merge-safety wave PR#614), events (the event stream from pm-e200), copy, and normalize. Everything else in the live census maps to an enumerated family or to the 2026-07-18 completion/test-all clarification. This is the exact regrowth property pm-wt43zj exists to prevent, observed against the table rather than against a command count: the migration tasks each move a known fragment, but a noun with no declared home will either fail the conformance gate or force the gate to be written around it. Placing these five is a prerequisite for wt43zj, not a follow-up to it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T17:08:30.429Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "## Placement proposal for the five unplaced core nouns (2026-07-26)\n\nThe target surface in this item's body enumerates roughly fifteen nouns and was written before graph, merge and events shipped. Five live top-level spellings have no placement, and pm-wt43zj records that placement is a prerequisite for the conformance gate — the gate cannot assert a table that does not cover the surface. Live surface today: 87 command entries, 89 top-level spellings, 326 declared flags across 52 contract flag lists. This note proposes a disposition for each of the five so ADR pm-pbyu has something concrete to accept or reject. No spelling is removed by any option below; every current invocation survives as an alias.\n\n**graph — promote to a canonical noun (the sixteenth).** It is the only workspace-scoped relationship query surface, it already carries eleven subcommands in one verb slot, and its subcommand enum is single-sourced in enum-contracts.ts. Folding it under the item facets group is wrong because facets are item-scoped and graph is workspace-scoped. Folding it under context is wrong because context assembles a bounded answer while graph answers structural questions about the whole workspace. Promotion costs one noun and buys a clean scope boundary.\n\n**events — fold into history as the workspace-scoped stream.** history is already the item-scoped mutation stream, and pm-tqel is consolidating redact, repair, compact, activity and restore beneath it. events is the same substance at workspace scope, so the spelling \"pm history events\" makes the scope difference explicit and removes a top-level noun. It also puts the cursor semantics of events next to the replay semantics of restore, which is where a reader looks for them.\n\n**copy — fold into create as a source flag.** copy is create with lifecycle fields reset and a copied_from provenance stamp, which is a creation mode rather than a distinct domain. The spelling \"pm create --from <id>\" states that relationship in the grammar instead of hiding it behind a separate verb, and it composes with the create-mode flags that already exist.\n\n**normalize — fold into the ops diagnostics family, and disposition its overlap with validate at the same time.** normalize plans and applies lifecycle metadata sweeps. validate --auto-fix --fix-scope plans and applies field backfills. These are two planners over one problem and the duplication is acknowledged nowhere. The placement decision should therefore be taken together with a decision about whether one of them is the planner and the other its selector, rather than moving a command and leaving the overlap in place.\n\n**merge — remove from the agent-facing table entirely as a machine-facing integration point.** The driver subcommand is invoked by git with a fixed argument vector recorded in .gitattributes, so renaming or nesting it is a breaking change to every installed clone for no agent benefit, and the install subcommand is a one-time workspace setup step. The honest disposition is that merge belongs to a non-agent tier of the surface rather than to the noun table — exactly the distinction pm-kxci8x owns. This makes the table both smaller and truer.\n\nNet effect if accepted: the noun table gains graph and loses copy, events, normalize and merge, so the canonical agent-facing surface shrinks while shipped capability is unchanged. The tiering decision for merge is the only part that depends on another open item."
    edited_at: "2026-07-26T17:09:06.972Z"
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T04:18:53.241Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification is being measured on the smallest of its three axes (2026-07-27 brainstorm, extends the placement proposal rather than replacing it).\n\nThis epic's acceptance criteria and its target-surface table are both expressed in nouns: roughly fifteen, one help screen. The 2026-07-26 census then found five core nouns with no home and a placement proposal followed. All of that is right and none of it is what an agent actually pays. Measured today against pm 2026.7.27, the surface an agent must learn has three axes, and the noun count is the smallest one.\n\nAxis one, spellings: 89 top-level entries, 152 commands and 126 actions in the contract enumeration. This is the axis the target table governs.\n\nAxis two, flags: 326 declared flags across 52 contract flag lists. Nothing in the target table constrains this, and it is where the real growth is. Two of the fifteen target nouns already carry more flags than the entire top-level command list has entries: pm create declares enough that its flags-only contract is 5994 bytes and pm update 6721. Consolidating list-open, list-closed and their six siblings into pm list --status moves complexity from axis one to axis two, which is the correct move for discoverability and a null move for the amount an agent must learn unless the flag lexicon is bounded at the same time. That is what pm-fs8q9x exists for, and the sequencing consequence is that a noun consolidation that lands before a flag lexicon can make the measured learning cost worse while making the help screen shorter.\n\nAxis three, output shapes: one per command, and none of them is declared anywhere an agent can read before paying for it. This is the axis with no owner in the target table at all, and it is the one that determines whether the second call an agent makes is cheap. An agent that has learned pm list still cannot predict what pm list returns, so it learns by paying.\n\nThe measurement that ties the three together, and the reason this note exists rather than a general observation about complexity: the call AGENTS.md designates for learning the surface, pm contracts --json, returns 125193 bytes, about 31000 tokens, and it is the brief form. It reports command_flags_omitted_reason, schema_omitted_reason and commander_aliases_omitted_reason all set to unfiltered_default_brief, so 31k tokens buys the spellings and neither the flags nor the shapes. Learning the flags for nine core commands costs about 29000 further bytes across nine more calls. The summary form is 5966 bytes. Filed as pm-gmdzaa, which is scoped to the cold-start entry point rather than to the ceiling contract that pm-5t33or owns.\n\nWhat this implies for the epic, offered for the ADR to accept or reject rather than asserted:\n\nFirst, the acceptance criteria should state a target on all three axes, because a fifteen-noun surface with 326 unconstrained flags satisfies the current wording while leaving the agent cost roughly unchanged. The existing criterion that agent token cost of pm --help plus contracts is measurably reduced is the right instinct and the right instrument; it is simply not connected to the noun target that the rest of the criteria describe.\n\nSecond, the ordering matters and currently runs the wrong way. The migration sequence recorded in the earlier note moves fragments noun by noun, with the conformance gates flipping from baseline to enforce at the end. If the flag lexicon is not bounded before the list family and the lifecycle aliases fold into their base nouns, each fold adds flags to a noun that already has many, and the gate at the end will be asserting a table whose second axis has grown throughout the migration. Bounding the lexicon first costs nothing that the folds need and makes each fold measurable.\n\nThird, the third axis needs an owner before the grammar is frozen. A declared output shape per command is what lets an agent decide whether to make a call, and it is the missing half of the contract that pm contracts already half-provides: the request side is declared, the response side is not. This is adjacent to the projection work in pm-cxr0jb but not the same thing, because an intent projection selects among shapes that are themselves undeclared.\n\nNothing here changes the placement proposal or the noun table. The claim is narrower: the epic will pass its own acceptance criteria while the thing it exists to fix, that the CLI is too complicated for agents, is only partly addressed, unless the flag lexicon and the output-shape declaration are inside the definition of done rather than beside it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T12:57:18.340Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface measurement 2026-07-27: 89 top-level command spellings on the installed build, against this epic's target of roughly 15 nouns on one help screen.\n\nCurrent list, for the record: activity, aggregate, append, beads, cal, calendar, changelog, claim, close, close-many, close-task, command-kit, comments, comments-audit, completion, completion-statuses, completion-tags, completion-types, config, context, contracts, copy, create, dedupe-audit, dedupe-merge, delete, deps, docs, duplicates, eval, event, events, extension, files, focus, gc, get, graph, guide, health, help, history, history-author-acknowledge, history-compact, history-redact, history-repair, init, install, item, learnings, list, list-all, list-blocked, list-canceled, list-closed, list-draft, list-in-progress, list-open, meet, merge, next, normalize, notes, package, pause-task, plan, pm, profile, reindex, release, remind, restore, schema, search, search-advanced, start-task, stats, telemetry, templates, test, test-all, test-runs, todos, twin, update, update-many, upgrade, validate, vcs.\n\nTwo structural observations that bear on the consolidation, beyond the raw count.\n\nThe seven list-* spellings and the four completion-* spellings are the obvious folds, and the note on this item already records that folding list-* into list --status is a null move unless the flag lexicon lands first. The same caveat applies to completion-*.\n\nThe less obvious one is that the surface is not one grammar but several stacked. Package-owned commands (beads, todos, twin, vcs, eval, search-advanced, normalize, reindex, command-kit, dedupe-audit, dedupe-merge, comments-audit) sit in the same namespace as core verbs and are indistinguishable in help output, yet they exit 2 with unknown command on a bare install. An agent reading the help of one installation learns a vocabulary that is wrong for another. Any consolidation that produces a one-screen surface has to decide whether extension-contributed commands appear on that screen at all, which is the tiering question pm-kxci8x owns; this note records the dependency rather than re-deciding it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T02:55:47.858Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Usage-weighted consolidation evidence, measured 2026-07-28 over the complete recorded history of this workspace: 40,343 entries across 2,155 streams, 37 distinct recorded ops.\n\nThe surface is wide but the usage is extremely concentrated. Cumulative share by op:\n\n  update                  25.83%   cum  25.83\n  comment_add             18.65%   cum  44.48\n  files_add               15.48%   cum  59.96\n  tests_add                7.22%   cum  67.18\n  claim                    6.37%   cum  73.55\n  create                   5.24%   cum  78.78\n  close                    5.21%   cum  83.99\n  release                  5.20%   cum  89.20\n  docs_add                 3.31%   cum  92.51\n  test_run_track           2.01%   cum  94.52\n  note_add                 1.62%   cum  96.14\n  learning_add             1.47%   cum  97.61\n  update_audit             0.77%   cum  98.37\n\nTHIRTEEN ops account for 99.4 percent of every mutation this project has ever recorded. The remaining 24 ops share 0.6 percent, and eleven of them have fewer than ten occurrences each across the entire corpus: plan_link_step 4, note_edit 4, comment_edit 3, comment_delete 3, plan_unlink_step 1, note_delete 1, plan_reorder_step 1, plan_approve 6, plan_append_decision 7, history_redact 23, delete 10.\n\nThree consequences for the consolidation design, all of which change the shape of the proposal:\n\n1. The tail is not dead weight to be removed, it is rarely-reached depth. Every one of those low-count ops corresponds to a real capability the user has forbidden removing. So the lever is REACHABILITY COST, not surface count: the eleven sub-ten ops should cost nothing to an agent that never needs them, and should be one predictable step away for the agent that does. That is an argument for depth-on-demand under a noun rather than for more top-level spellings, and it is a measured argument rather than an aesthetic one.\n\n2. The annotation family is the single largest consolidation prize and is currently four separate top-level spellings. comment_add, note_add, learning_add and the files/docs/tests link adds together are 46.7 percent of all recorded mutations. They share one shape: attach a typed payload to an item. They are spelled inconsistently even in the record - files_add, docs_add and tests_add are plural while note_add, comment_add and learning_add are singular. Consolidating the annotation verbs is worth more measured agent traffic than consolidating the entire list-star family.\n\n3. update at 25.8 percent is the catch-all and the single most valuable target for INTENT SPLITTING rather than merging. One quarter of all recorded mutations go through a verb whose meaning is only recoverable by reading its patch. Any relevance, forecasting or analytics mechanism that reads op as a signal gets almost no information from the most common op in the corpus.\n\nSurface census on the same build for the record: pm --help renders 89 top-level command spellings; pm contracts --summary enumerates 75. That 14-command gap is now its own defect and is not a consolidation question - see the enumeration-closure issue filed 2026-07-28.\n\nMethod note so this is reproducible and never re-derived by hand: read every .agents/pm/history/*.jsonl, count the entry-level op field. Do NOT grep for op in the raw file - the JSON-patch entries inside the patch array also carry an op key, and a naive grep returns replace 118,975 and add 89,013, which are patch operations and not commands."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T15:07:11.773Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification lever, recorded 2026-07-28: the surface an agent must reason about is not only the commands and flags, it is the vocabulary, and the vocabulary contains synonyms that are not synonyms.\n\nEvery lever on this item so far has counted tokens of surface — 89 top-level spellings, 326 flags across 52 lists, help enumerating 89 while contracts enumerates 75, the reachability cost of finding a command at all. All of them measure how much an agent must read. This pass measured something different: how many ways an agent can correctly say one thing, and whether the product agrees with itself about what was said.\n\nFor the single most common relation in any tracker, one item must finish before another, there are three ways to write it and three different outcomes. A blocked_by row on the dependent is read by the scheduler and by the graph engine. A blocks row on the blocker is read by the graph engine and ignored by the scheduler, and it is the more common of the two on disk, 219 rows against 127. Writing both, which is what an author does the moment they discover the split, makes pm validate report a dependency cycle — verified live this pass by adding the pair and watching graph audit go 11 findings to 12 and a new warning class appear, then reverting. Fifteen dependency-kind spellings are accepted and only depends_on canonicalizes on write; the rest are stored verbatim and canonicalized on read, so the disk and the reader disagree by design.\n\nWhy this belongs on this item rather than only on pm-4020c5. The lexicon item owns the decision about what the kinds mean. This item owns the claim that the surface is too complicated for agents, and a choice with three spellings, one silent failure and no diagnostic is exactly that complexity in its most expensive form: the agent cannot discover the difference from help, cannot detect it from the exit code, and pays for it later as a scheduling error attributed to the tracker rather than to the write. Reducing 89 commands to a smaller noun-verb table does nothing about it. Collapsing the ordering vocabulary to one direction removes a real decision from every agent's path without removing any capability, because both spellings express exactly the same fact.\n\nThe generalization worth carrying into the noun-verb work: for each relation and each field the surface accepts, count the number of distinct ways to record the same fact, and treat any count above one as surface to be removed rather than flexibility to be preserved. Aliases that map to one stored form are free — that is what pm-0z7n's shim layer provides. Spellings that map to different stored forms with different downstream readings are the opposite of an alias, and the fifteen-kind vocabulary contains at least one such pair today.\n\nSecond datapoint from the same pass, on cost rather than vocabulary. The orientation sequence AGENTS.md tells every agent to run before creating work — context --limit 10, search, list-open --limit 20, list-in-progress --limit 20 — costs 16,631 bytes on this 2,172-item tracker, and adding next --limit 5 brings the mandated preamble to 25,532 bytes, roughly 6,400 tokens spent before any work is touched. Root help is another 12,963 bytes if the agent needs to find a command. The same context call in JSON is 10,277 bytes against 7,517 in TOON, 37 percent more, which is the measurable basis for the TOON-first guidance. Owner for the sequence-versus-single-call question is pm-cxr0jb; recorded here because it quantifies the claim this item exists to fix."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T04:46:40.508Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface census 2026-07-29, measured against the live build, and the consolidation thesis that follows from it.\n\nThe numbers. 84 top-level commands render 1,792 flag occurrences drawn from 481 distinct flag spellings. Median flags per command is 11.5, mean 21.3, maximum 126, minimum 1. Two hundred spellings, 41.6 percent of the distinct vocabulary, appear on exactly one command. Fifty-four spellings appear on ten or more. The ten heaviest commands by flag count are update-many 126, plan 97, update 84, create 78, list 65, list-all 64, and list-open, list-in-progress, list-draft and list-closed at 63 each.\n\nDiscovery cost, three rungs. Root help is 13,075 bytes, about 3,268 tokens. All 84 per-command helps together are 212,496 bytes, about 53,124 tokens. Machine-readable contracts default to 11,843 bytes, about 2,960 tokens, and enumerate 70 commands. A single command's flag contract via contracts --command get --flags-only is 939 bytes. The full machine-readable surface, contracts --full --json, is 1,142,247 bytes, about 285,561 tokens.\n\nRead those together and the discovery ladder is already mostly right. The middle rung is excellent: 939 bytes to learn one command exactly. The entry rung is cheap at 2,960 tokens but enumerates 70 of 84, so fourteen commands are invisible to the only affordable index. The top rung does not exist as a usable artifact: 285,561 tokens exceeds every context window in service, so no agent has ever read the complete declared surface and no agent ever will.\n\nThe thesis. The surface is not hard because there are 84 commands. Eighty-four nouns is a shell utility, not a hazard. It is hard because 481 flag spellings encode a much smaller number of concepts, and 200 of those spellings are reachable only by reading one specific command's help, which means they cannot be learned by pattern and must be memorized one at a time. Command count is the visible metric and the wrong one. Reducing 84 nouns to 12 while leaving 481 spellings in place would move the tax rather than remove it, which is the same conclusion the earlier note reached from the reachability angle and which this census now quantifies.\n\nCollapse one, the filter predicate. Eight list-family commands carry 63 to 65 flags each. They differ from one another in a single status literal. Search, next, context, aggregate, duplicates, test-all, update-many and close-many each accept an overlapping subset of the same filter vocabulary, and the subsets are different in ways no rule predicts. If the predicate is declared once as a contract object and every query surface consumes it, an agent learns filtering once. The eight list commands survive as aliases that prefill one field of that predicate, so no invocation an agent already knows stops working and nothing is deleted. The measurable outcome is that the number of distinct filter spellings falls while the number of accepting commands rises.\n\nCollapse two, the item field set. Create declares 78 flags, update 84, update-many 126. That is 288 of 1,792 occurrences, sixteen percent of the entire surface, spent re-declaring one item schema three times. The divergences between the three are exactly what agents trip on and what the gotcha ledger is full of: repeated --ac accumulates on update, --add-ac and --remove-ac exist on update alone, create has no description-file input while annotation commands have --file, and a semicolon inside an acceptance criterion is rejected on one path and irrelevant on another. Those are not independent bugs; they are the predictable result of writing the same field domain three times by hand. One declared field-set contract with per-verb applicability makes divergence structurally impossible instead of merely documented, and it is the precondition for pm-xm7c's batch mutation input to be specifiable at all.\n\nCollapse three, classify the singletons. A flag used by one command is not automatically wrong. A flag used by one command that an agent cannot distinguish from a shared one is always wrong, because the agent's only recovery is to try it elsewhere and read an error. Every one of the 200 singletons should carry a declared disposition: local, with a stated reason it does not generalize, or shared, in which case it is actually shared. That converts a 200-item memorization tax into a 200-row table readable once, and it gives the flag lexicon gate on pm-fs8q9x an unambiguous acceptance target rather than a judgement call per flag.\n\nWhat this says about sequencing. The list-family fold has been described before as a null move without flag governance, and the census confirms it precisely: folding eight commands into one removes eight rows from a help index and zero spellings from the lexicon. Collapse two and collapse three are the load-bearing work, and both are flag-lexicon work, which places pm-fs8q9x upstream of the visible noun consolidation rather than beside it. The noun table on pm-pbyu remains correct as the eventual shape; it is simply not the first tranche.\n\nTwo contract defects the census exposes on the way. The affordable index enumerates 70 of 84 commands, which is the enumeration gap already owned elsewhere, now with a current count. And the complete form is 285,561 tokens, which is not a large payload but an unusable one; a complete surface that no consumer can load is indistinguishable from an absent one, and it should be paged or queryable rather than emitted whole."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T16:58:31.063Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification extension 2026-07-29: placing the seven spellings that landed after the noun table, and a three-pattern classification that makes the fold mechanical.\n\nWhy an extension and not a restart. The ADR pm-pbyu holds the complete spelling-to-noun table with its twelve target nouns, its fourteen permanent bare-verb aliases and its five open rulings. Nothing here revises that table. This note supplies only what the table cannot answer yet, and one new metric.\n\nThe surface at build 2026.7.29. Rendered top-level spellings in root help: 87. Commands enumerated by contracts summary: 71. Seven spellings landed after the table was written: workspace, eval, item, duplicates, start-task, pause-task, close-task. All seven enumerate and resolve correctly, so this is not a contract gap; it is a placement gap. It is also the third time the table has been outrun - first by graph, merge, events, copy and normalize, then by workspace, now by these seven - and each time placement had to be re-derived by hand from live help. The recurrence is the argument for the conformance gate: a new top-level spelling with no declared noun placement should fail the check, which turns placement into a build-time obligation instead of a review-time discovery.\n\nA three-pattern classification of the redundancy. Every spelling that is not already a canonical noun-verb pair falls into exactly one of three patterns, and each pattern has exactly one mechanical fold with its own prerequisite.\n\nPattern A, the value is in the name. Seven list spellings are the same read with a status value baked into the command name: list-draft, list-open, list-in-progress, list-blocked, list-closed, list-canceled, list-all. The fold is one noun with the value moved to a flag. Prerequisite: the flag lexicon, because the fold is a null move while the filter flags disagree on value grammar - status accepts comma-separated values, type and priority reject them, and tag silently matches the literal comma-joined string.\n\nPattern B, a subcommand flattened into the name. Four history spellings - redact, repair, author-acknowledge, compact - plus reindex and normalize. These already belong to a noun that exists; hyphenation is the only difference between the current spelling and the target. Consolidation is tracked as pm-tqel.\n\nPattern C, composition shipped as a new spelling. start-task, pause-task and close-task are each two existing verbs run in sequence, and the help text says so outright: claim an item and move it to in_progress; move an item to open and release its claim; close an item and release assignment metadata. These three are the strongest evidence yet that what the surface needs is a declared composition mechanism, not more names. Three composites cost three spellings; the same ergonomic win for any other useful pair costs another one, forever.\n\nWhy the classification matters. It converts the single complaint that the surface is too large into three separable decisions with three different prerequisites, and only Pattern C requires machinery that does not exist. Patterns A and B are pure renaming behind aliases and can land as soon as their prerequisites do.\n\nPlacement proposal for the seven, as input to the ADR rather than a ruling.\n\nworkspace stays a noun. It is the only home for whole-tracker operations and its first verb group, snapshot, is correctly a subcommand rather than a spelling. Carry the durability finding pm-6l2mza with it: the restore verb is the most consequential mutation in the product and currently the least recorded.\n\neval should not stand alone. It is one measurement of one subsystem, and it belongs with the other two commands that answer some version of is-this-workspace-sound, validate and health. If the ruling keeps it top-level, it should state why a measurement is a noun, because the same argument then admits a top-level spelling for every future measurement.\n\nitem needs a ruling before it grows. Today item mutate applies create, update and close atomically, while create, update and close exist as bare verbs doing the same thing singly. Either item is the batch namespace and the bare verbs are its singular sugar, or the bare verbs are the item namespace and atomic application is a flag on them. Both are defensible; having both spellings with neither declared canonical is the failure mode the table exists to prevent.\n\nduplicates is a read over items with a similarity predicate and belongs as a mode of the read noun that owns list and search. Its discoverability matters more than its name, because it is the only surface that satisfies the duplicate-check obligation this repository's own agent rules impose before creating work.\n\nstart-task, pause-task and close-task should not be placed. They should be the first consumers of composition.\n\nA fourth simplification axis, new this session. The metric that matters to an agent is not the number of flags but the number of distinct pairs of spelling and concept, which is strictly larger. Proof: --file is item data on create and update, where the value is a linked path stored on the item, and an input channel on comments, notes and learnings, where the value is a file the prose is read out of and the path itself is never stored. Both meanings are the documented recommendation for their own family, and generalising either one to the other fails silently. Recorded in full on the flag-lexicon item. This axis composes with the axis already recorded here - the number of distinct ways to record one fact - and both are counts an agent pays for in learning cost rather than in tokens.\n\nWhat this note does not change. The twelve-noun target, the fourteen bare-verb aliases and the five open rulings stand. No spelling is proposed for removal, and every current spelling survives as a hidden alias under the lexicon's alias criterion, so the zero-feature-loss constraint is untouched."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T21:26:11.202Z"
    author: "harness:codex"
    text: "Execution blueprint for command simplification\n\nThe target is a small noun-first grammar whose permanent concepts are item, context, history, graph, schema, config, package, workspace, test, plan, ops, and help/contracts. Existing bare verbs remain hidden compatibility aliases where shortening is materially valuable. All feature behavior survives; consolidation changes placement and discovery, not capability.\n\nTransition gates: decide the complete placement table in pm-pbyu; ship the alias resolver and migration hint contract in pm-0z7n; migrate command families behind aliases; generate CLI help, shell completion, MCP actions, SDK action schemas, and documentation from the same contract registry; replay old agent transcripts byte-for-byte where compatibility is promised; measure tokens and wrong-command retries before and after; freeze removal policy only after usage counters show an alias is unused.\n\nToken contract: default help is one screen; contracts summary is one bounded row per noun; command-scoped flags are one further bounded call; errors return the attempted intent, the nearest valid spelling, the corrected invocation, and no unrelated catalog. Mutation output defaults to id, status, and changed-field count. Read output is intent-scoped, streamable, jq-safe, and explicit about truncation.\n\nShell composability remains a first-class acceptance boundary: stable exit codes; stdout contains data only; stderr contains diagnostics only; JSON, TOON, NDJSON, CSV, and table modes preserve information-equivalent fields where claimed; every list supports filtering, projection, ordering, pagination, and streaming without loading the whole workspace."
    edited_at: "2026-07-29T21:27:47.558Z"
  - created_at: "2026-07-30T09:25:08.666Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification axis, 2026-07-30: count the number of independent VOCABULARIES an agent must learn, not the number of commands it must choose between\n\nThe 07-28 axis counted the distinct ways to record one fact, which is write-side redundancy. This axis is its read-side twin and it measures worse. An agent operating pm must currently hold three non-nested command vocabularies for one capability set: 85 spellings rendered by help, 70 declared by the contract table, 86 in the MCP pm_run action enum plus 28 narrow tool names. Twenty-six spellings exist only in MCP and seven capability families only in the terminal. Learning one vocabulary does not let an agent predict another, which is the actual cost - not the count of any single one.\n\nThe multiplication continues inside a single command. One flag name can carry two value domains: --for means a declared read intent on get, list, next and search, and is validated against the context section enum on context, where the two declared intents are consequently unreachable. Paired flags for one concept can carry two grammars: --dep refuses the unprefixed comma form with an explicit message, while --dep-remove accepts it, matches nothing and exits 0. So the vocabulary count is not even stable per command; it varies per flag and per direction of a flag pair.\n\nConsequence for the consolidation plan. Folding the list-star family into list --status was already known to be a null move unless the flag lexicon lands first. This axis strengthens that to a general ordering claim: every noun consolidation is a null move for an MCP-driven agent, because the MCP vocabulary is not derived from the noun table and would keep the pre-consolidation spellings regardless of what the CLI renders. Consolidating 85 spellings into 12 nouns while pm_run keeps its own 86-entry enum produces a fourth vocabulary rather than removing three. The MCP action set must therefore be derived from the same table this epic consolidates, in the same slice, or the epic's own acceptance criterion - that the agent token cost of orientation measurably falls - is unreachable for the transport most agents actually use.\n\nMetric this axis proposes for the epic's evidence table: not the top-level command count, but the number of distinct declared vocabularies (one per surface, plus one per flag whose value domain is not shared across the commands that render it), and the size of the largest pairwise set difference between any two of them. Today those are at least four and 26 respectively. Zero and zero is the target, and unlike a command count it cannot be reached by hiding spellings behind aliases."
learnings[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-05T04:55:45.356Z",maintainer-agent,Surface growth pattern learned from history; features were added as new top-level commands because that was the path of least resistance in Commander registration — the grammar ADR plus contracts-generated help removes that incentive by making subcommand addition cheaper than top-level addition.
  "2026-07-06T08:04:42.231Z",maintainer-agent,"Dogfood measurements (2026-07-05 external agent audit, GH-435/437/442/443/445) put hard numbers on the simplification thesis: bootstrap costs ~25KB (pm contracts) before the first real command; per-loop costs are dominated by output shape (full-item mutation echoes 12.7x over --id-only; tags 43% of pm context; JSON +48-53% over TOON). Consolidating the grammar (this epic) fixes discovery cost; the pm-mpbb lean-output cluster fixes loop cost. Both are needed to hit the agent token-cost AC."
  "2026-07-25T12:41:51.550Z","harness:claude-code","An agent's cost of learning a CLI is the product of nouns, verbs, and flags, not the count of top-level commands. The consolidation programme measures success by shrinking roughly sixty top-level commands toward fifteen nouns, which is real, but pm create alone publishes more than sixty flags, and the same concept is spelled differently across neighbouring commands. Shrinking one factor while the others stay unconstrained moves the learning cost rather than removing it. Any future surface-simplification claim should be stated against all three factors and against measured help-token cost, not against the command count alone."
